  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 254 AM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nKeeping an eye on some developing fog this morning over parts of \nnortheastern South Dakota and western Minnesota. Skies will remain \nmostly clear over the next few hours, allowing for nearly full \nhumidity recovery over much of the forecast area. Generally light \nwind this morning at 5 miles per hour, gusting to 10 at the most. \nHowever, winds at this speed may be just strong enough to dissipate \nfor development over a lot of the region. In areas in which fog does \nmanage to form, generally expecting patchy coverage and overall \nminor visibility reductions. Should conditions line up perfectly, \nvisibility down as low as a mile could be possible, but confidence \nis low on that playing out in more than highly localized areas this \nmorning. \n\nPositively tilted upper-level ridge extending into the Northern \nPlains will lead to a temperature gradient across the forecast area \ntoday. Northeastern South Dakota and western Minnesota will maintain \nnear-normal temperatures today and tomorrow, while temperatures over \ncentral and north central South Dakota will return to the 90s, \nperhaps even touching 100 degrees on Sunday. This above normal heat, \ncombined with southerly winds gusting up to 20 to 30 miles per hour \nover that same area could lead to some elevated fire weather \nconcerns in that area. The only other forecast item of note today \nwill be wildfire smoke persisting over the region. Most of the smoke \nwill remain aloft, and only minor reductions to air quality, if any, \nat the surface are expected this weekend. \n\nPressure gradient tightens Sunday, leading to strong low-level flow \nover much of the region. Southerly gusts are expected to reach 30 to \n40 miles per hour by the afternoon from the James River Valley and \neast. The aforementioned above normal heat over central and north \ncentral South Dakota will help to push afternoon humidity down to \nbelow 30 percent at times, which when combined with the wind leads \nto elevated fire weather conditions. The main area of concern will \nbe counties west of the Missouri River. Red Flag Warning conditions \ndo not appear likely at this point, mainly due to humidity falling a \nbit short of the 20 percent criteria over that area. However, the \nGrassland Fire Danger Index will reach High to Very High levels \nregardless. \n\nEarly next week, an upper-level trough will develop and move over \nthe Northern Plains. A cold front will move through Monday afternoon \nthrough early Tuesday morning, along which storms are expected to \ndevelop. Severe chances do exist with this setup, and both hail and \nwind appear to potential severe threats with storms that develop. \nMUCAPE of roughly 1000-2000 J/kg is expected, along with shear of \n~40 knots and mid-level lapse rates approaching 8 C/km all \ncontributing to the hail threat. There is a bit more uncertainty in \nthe potential wind threat, but there is some signal in model \nguidance for DCAPE values over 1000 J/kg and low-level lapse rates \napproaching 8 C/km under an inverted-V sounding. Perhaps the biggest \nobstacle will be the capped setup, in place around the 700mb layer. \nThe cap is strongest over central South Dakota and weakens as you \nmove to the northeast. The forcing from the cold front may help to \nbreak the cap where it is weak, meaning that the most likely area \nfor severe storm development will be over northeast South Dakota and \nwestern Minnesota. The Storm Prediction Center has placed a Marginal \nRisk over much of this area, with a Slight Risk clipping far \nnortheastern South Dakota as well as Traverse County. \n\nBehind the trough, uncertainty on the upper-level pattern increases \nsignificantly. Ensemble guidance shows some potential signal for \nanother ridge to build in, but the question remains of how deep that \nridge will be able to build. Highest probability within ensemble \nclusters trends towards a solution in which the Northern Plains ends \nup on the front side of the weak ridge, perhaps under a weak cold \nair advection regime aloft. Regardless of the exact setup, there is \nsome confidence in temperatures trending back up towards the 80s to \nlow 90s as the trough moves out.",
